rms"], "rationale": "Utah's deep Republican lean, with no polling, ratings, or candidate infrastructure pointing to a competitive race, keeps Republicans clear favorites. Coverage suggests the state's GOP remains healthy beyond Trump personally, though a weak national environment bears watching.", "watch": "Early 2028 polling, the eventual nominees, and whether the national environment that has Trump positive in only ten states persists into the general election could tighten or widen this race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Utah enters the 2028 presidential race as a Republican stronghold, with an R+11 partisan lean and no incumbent on the ballot. No general-election polls or expert ratings are available yet, so the outlook rests on the state's fundamentals. Recent coverage highlights Utah's evolving politics — Democratic hopefuls are courting the state and local outlets debate whether it is trending blue — but the same reporting notes that potential Republican successors to Trump poll better among Utahns than the president himself.\n\nThe national picture adds some uncertainty: the president's approval is positive in only about ten states heading into the midterms, and Utah could prove vulnerable if that environment deepens. Still, with no Democratic campaign infrastructure, no fundraising data on either side, and no polling showing movement, Republicans remain clear favorites to carry Utah's six electoral votes. Early 2028 polling will be the first meaningful test of whether the state is genuinely shifting.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1067, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1478, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1592, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "